# Creational, Structural, and Behavioral Design Patterns

## 1. Introduction & Learning Objectives

**Subject:** Object-Oriented Programming Systems | **Chapter:** Object-Oriented Design Patterns

**Prerequisites:** solid-design-principles

### What is this topic and why does it matter?
Design patterns are battle-tested architectural templates for recurring software problems. We explore the GoF taxonomy: Creational (Singleton, Factory, Builder), Structural (Adapter, Decorator, Facade), and Behavioral (Observer, Strategy, Command).

### Specific Learning Outcomes
- **Creational Patterns**: Control object creation mechanics (Singleton, Factory, Builder).
- **Structural Patterns**: Compose classes and interfaces cleanly (Adapter, Decorator, Facade).
- **Behavioral Patterns**: Orchestrate communication and algorithms (Observer, Strategy, Command).

## 2. Intuitive Explanation & Mental Model

Think of building construction. Architects do not invent how to frame a doorway or wire a three-way light switch from scratch for every building; they use standardized blueprints that solve specific structural challenges reliably.

## 3. Deep-Dive Theory & Core Mechanics

### 1. Creational Patterns
- **Singleton:** Ensures a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access. (Modern C++: *Meyers’ Singleton* using static local).
- **Factory Method:** Defines an interface for creating an object, but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate.
- **Builder:** Separates the construction of a complex object from its representation.

### 2. Structural Patterns
- **Adapter:** Converts the interface of a class into another interface clients expect (wrapper).
- **Decorator:** Attaches additional responsibilities to an object dynamically without subclassing.
- **Facade:** Provides a unified, simplified high-level interface to a complex subsystem.

### 3. Behavioral Patterns
- **Strategy:** Defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable at runtime.
- **Observer:** Defines a one-to-many dependency so that when one object changes state, all its dependents are notified automatically.
- **Command:** Encapsulates a request as an object, thereby letting you parameterize clients with queues, logs, and undo operations.

## 4. Progressively Worked Examples

### Example 1: Meyers’ Singleton & Strategy Pattern in Modern C++ (Standard)

**Problem Statement:** Implement a thread-safe Singleton Logger and interchangeable Strategy compressors.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

// 1. Thread-safe Meyers' Singleton
class Logger {
public:
    static Logger& getInstance() {
        static Logger instance; // Thread-safe in C++11 and later
        return instance;
    }
    void log(const std::string& msg) {
        std::cout << "[LOG]: " << msg << "\n";
    }
private:
    Logger() = default;
    Logger(const Logger&) = delete;
    Logger& operator=(const Logger&) = delete;
};

// 2. Strategy Pattern
class ICompressionStrategy {
public:
    virtual ~ICompressionStrategy() = default;
    virtual void compress(const std::string& file) = 0;
};

class ZipCompression : public ICompressionStrategy {
public:
    void compress(const std::string& file) override {
        std::cout << "Compressing " << file << " using ZIP\n";
    }
};

class RarCompression : public ICompressionStrategy {
public:
    void compress(const std::string& file) override {
        std::cout << "Compressing " << file << " using RAR\n";
    }
};

class FileArchiver {
private:
    std::unique_ptr<ICompressionStrategy> strategy;
public:
    void setStrategy(std::unique_ptr<ICompressionStrategy> s) {
        strategy = std::move(s);
    }
    void archive(const std::string& file) {
        if (strategy) strategy->compress(file);
    }
};
```

**Detailed Explanation & Tracing:**
Meyers Singleton uses C++11 magic statics guaranteeing thread-safe one-time initialization. The Strategy pattern allows FileArchiver to switch compression engines on the fly.

## 5. Production & Practical Engineering Applications

Logging engines, UI event listeners, payment routing, HTTP middleware pipelines, and document rendering pipelines.

## 6. Visual Diagrams & Architectural Representation

Pattern Categories:
Creational: [ Singleton ]  [ Factory Method ]  [ Builder ]
Structural: [ Adapter ]    [ Decorator ]       [ Facade ]
Behavioral: [ Observer ]   [ Strategy ]        [ Command ]

## 7. Comparative Analysis & Engineering Trade-Offs

### Strategy vs State Pattern

| Aspect | Strategy Pattern | State Pattern |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Intent** | Swap algorithms independently of client | Alter behavior as internal state transitions |
| **Awareness** | Strategies usually don't know about each other | Concrete states often trigger transitions to next state |
| **Client Control** | Client typically chooses the strategy | Transitions happen automatically based on events |

## 8. Common Pitfalls & Antipatterns

### Pitfall 1: Overusing the Singleton Pattern as a hidden global variable
- **Why developers fail here:** Singletons introduce tight coupling, hide class dependencies, and make unit testing with mocks extremely difficult.
- **Concrete Counterexample / Symptom:** Test cases cannot run in parallel or bleed state into subsequent tests.
- **How to prevent / correct:** Pass dependencies through constructors (Dependency Injection) instead of reaching out to global Singletons.

## 9. Interview Questions & Comprehensive Answers

### Q1: How is thread safety guaranteed in Meyers’ Singleton in C++11? (Language & Concurrency)

**Model Answer:**
According to the C++11 standard (§6.7 [stmt.dcl]), if control enters the declaration of a block-scope static variable concurrently while the variable is being initialized, the concurrent execution shall wait for completion of the initialization. The compiler generates implicit thread synchronization (guard variables) around the static local variable.

*Interviewer Follow-up:* "What is the Decorator pattern and how does it prevent class explosion?"

*Follow-up Response:* Decorator wraps an existing component inside an identical interface, adding dynamic behaviors at runtime. Without it, combining features (e.g., SimpleCoffee, MilkCoffee, SugarMilkCoffee, WhipSugarMilkCoffee) causes an exponential explosion of static subclasses.

## 10. Rapid Revision Cheat Sheet

- **Meyers Singleton:** static Logger instance; thread-safe one-time init
- **Factory Method:** Delegates creation to subclasses
- **Adapter:** Translates interface A to interface B
- **Observer:** Publish-subscribe notification model

## 11. Self-Assessment Mastery Checklist

- [ ] Can implement thread-safe Singleton and explain magic statics
- [ ] Can implement Strategy, Observer, and Factory patterns in C++
- [ ] Can identify when a design pattern introduces needless complexity
